---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { thesis, milestones, references } from '../data/research';
import { withBase } from '../lib/urls';

const statusLabel = { done: 'Done', 'in-progress': 'In progress', planned: 'Next' } as const;
const [cvit, cpg, sema] = references;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Research"
  description="Undergraduate thesis research by Bach Nguyen on continual learning for CascadedViT, an efficient vision transformer, in the Visual Computing Lab at Ontario Tech University."
  type="article"
>
  <p class="article-meta">Undergraduate thesis · {thesis.lab} · {thesis.start} – present</p>
  <h1 class="page-title">Continual Learning for CascadedViT</h1>
  <p class="lead muted">{thesis.summary} Supervised by {thesis.supervisor}.</p>
  <div class="article-links">
    <a href={cvit.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">CascadedViT paper (arXiv) →</a>
  </div>

  <article class="prose">
    <h2>The problem: catastrophic forgetting</h2>
    <p>Train a network on one task, then fine-tune it on a second, and its accuracy on the first usually collapses. The gradients for the new task overwrite the weights that encoded the old one. This is <strong>catastrophic forgetting</strong>.</p>
    <p>The simple fix is to retrain on all the data every time something new arrives. That means storing every past example and paying for a full training run on each update, which doesn't work when data arrives over time, can't be kept, or the model lives on a small device. Continual learning looks for ways to learn a sequence of tasks while keeping what the model already knows. The core tension is <em>stability</em> (don't forget) against <em>plasticity</em> (still learn new things).</p>

    <h2>Why CascadedViT</h2>
    <p><a href={cvit.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">CascadedViT (CViT)</a>, by Srivathsan Sivakumar and Faisal Qureshi, is a vision transformer built for compute- and battery-constrained hardware. Its Cascaded-Chunk Feed-Forward Network (CCFFN) splits features into chunks to cut parameters and FLOPs. In the paper, CViT-XL reaches 75.5% top-1 on ImageNet-1K with 15% fewer FLOPs and 3.3% less energy than EfficientViT-M5.</p>
    <p>The phones and drones CViT is designed for are also where continual learning matters most. A deployed model should pick up new classes on the device, where retraining from scratch or storing all past data isn't realistic. But most continual learning work has been done on larger backbones, and a compact model has less spare capacity to share between tasks.</p>

    <h2>What I'm working on</h2>
    <p>My thesis asks: <strong>{thesis.question.charAt(0).toLowerCase() + thesis.question.slice(1)}</strong></p>
    <p>Keeping old knowledge usually costs something, whether that's extra parameters, extra compute, or stored data. For a model whose whole point is efficiency, those costs matter as much as accuracy. I'm working with two published methods:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong><a href={cpg.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">CPG</a></strong> (Compacting, Picking and Growing; Hung et al., NeurIPS 2019) avoids forgetting by construction. After each task, the weights it used are pruned and the survivors are frozen. Later tasks train only free weights, can reuse frozen ones through a learned mask, and add capacity only when the free pool runs out. It was designed for convolutional networks, so part of the work is adapting it to a hybrid transformer.</li>
      <li><strong><a href={sema.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">SEMA</a></strong> (Wang et al.) adds small adapter modules as experts to a pre-trained model, uses a router to decide which experts handle an input, and adds new adapters only when the data distribution shifts. I'm working on the routing mechanism.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Progress</h2>
    <ul>
      {milestones.map((m) => (
        <li><strong>{statusLabel[m.status]}: {m.title}.</strong> {m.body}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p>CascadedViT, CPG and SEMA are the work of their authors. My part is implementing and adapting them for CascadedViT and running the experiments, with my supervisor and a CascadedViT co-author.</p>
    <p>This work is ongoing, and I'll share results here once they're ready to be made public. Before the thesis, I explored a related idea in my <a href={withBase('/projects/nested-learning/')}>Nested Learning</a> project.</p>

    <h2>References</h2>
    <ol>
      {references.map((r) => (
        <li>{r.authors}. <a href={r.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{r.title}</a>. {r.venue}.</li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
